so it's come to my attention that ned  from the try guys has a lot of problems  with his knee being an osteopathic  physician one who has some extra  training in sports medicine i'm excited  to watch this video let's get into it  in fall of 2012 i broke my knee so bad  that it was cut down to the bone and my  friend said this is the day that we saw  ned's kneecap what was cut down i want  to know was his skin like open when  people say break knee i don't know if  it's a fracture of their femur their  tibia their patella which is just the  kneecap it was playing paintball in the  woods in rural new hampshire it's  something called a septic knee i cut my  knee down to the bone and then it got  infected oh okay so what happens in a  septic joint you get this bacterial  infection within the joint space the  knee starts swelling gets real hot it  gets real red what we do in order to  diagnose it actually outside of seeing  that it looks infectious is actually put  in a needle draw fluid out and send it  for testing to see the cell counts to  see if there's white blood cells present  which could be a sign of infection we  also sent a culture of that fluid where  we actually try and grow and cultivate  the bacteria to see what type of  bacteria is present and which  antibiotics it could be resistant to i  had multiple surgeries to clean it out  and my knee was completely destroyed i  was off work for months playing video  games all day it got to the point where  i i  just  taking painkillers was like the best  part of my day  and it was a really um  really scary time yeah it's really scary  because treatment options for  painkillers are quite limited you think  about over-the-counter options you have  your non-steroidal anti-inflammatories  like ibuprofen motrin then you have your  acetaminophen which is like your  tylenols of the world then after that  fairly limited the next line is really  opioid therapy if you have nerve pain  there's some other options like  gabapentin bronton and then we have like  superficial treatment options where you  have non-steroidal anti-inflammatory  enzyme gel like ulterior gel diclofenac  you also have lidocaine patches which  can help numb the area temporarily to  give you healing but long term  we don't have great ways of pain control  except allowing your body a chance to  heal itself oxycodone is an opioid drug  and that's one of the main  factors that's contributing to the  current opioid crisis so before we were  definitely over prescribing oxycodone  and this was like kind of happening  during my medical training where we  started to realize that we're over  treating pain this initially started  obviously with the pharmaceutical  companies playing games and misleading  doctors about whether or not these  medications were addicting but also  because of some of the surveys that we  were giving patients ask them questions  of whether or not their pain was treated  adequately and doctors were seeing  reimbursements fall meaning hospitals  weren't getting paid doctors weren't  being reimbursed for their care because  patients were saying i still had a level  of pain and as a result doctors started  over treating pain in order to get  better patient satisfaction scores but  giving the patients what they want fully  100 full stop may not actually be ideal  for their health a typical situation  could be like what happened to me you  have a horrible injury you're off work  start taking pain medications because  you're actually in pain but then you get  addicted to it so you continue taking  pain medications this isn't about the  opioid crisis in the united states it's  about me most people stop taking them  within six weeks of a surgery but here i  am three months later  still taking high doses of  oxycodone every single day so at some  point you need to figure out a step down  approach or figure out why the pain is  continuing is there something that  surgically needs to be addressed is it  about beginning some sort of rehab  protocol and a very interesting one that  a lot of people don't talk about taking  off opioids can actually reduce pain  kind of an interesting concept it's  paradoxical in nature over the last six  years i've still had lingering effects  anytime i would bend over my knee would  pop and it would be painful here i am  after soccer  icing my knee and it makes me think  that i'm not gonna be able to jump  around and play with my kids is it worth  it to willingly go back and get a  surgery just for the potential just the  possibility of having no pain so there's  a lot of options here there's injectable  options surgical options i know a lot of  people in the medical community like to  poo poo acupuncture and integrative  medicine i think that there is  definitely some value there i've  actually had good outcomes with it for a  patient who's having knee pain of his  age it's reasonable to say  maybe it's time that you don't play  soccer anymore because there's patients  of mine who are 40 years old have had  knee problems knee surgeries in the past  and are playing pickup basketball with  20 year olds and are constantly having  knee pain there's times where in life we  have to admit that maybe we can't do  that anymore and it doesn't mean that  life is terrible it means that  adaptations have to be made some  patients will say i refuse for that to  be the adaptation i'd rather try the  surgery and risk it and that's totally  fine as long as they're aware of those  risks  [Music]  he said that everything went really well  they cleaned out all the scar tissue he  also found a minor meniscus tear which  he fixed meniscus common word used a lot  of patients don't know what it is you  have a big femur bone at the top of your  thigh a slightly smaller bone the tibia  in your shin they sit on top of one  another in order for your knee to move  you need to have good mobility so you  have these two c-shaped pads called a  meniscus a lateral meniscus and a medial  meniscus one's on the outside ones on  the inside and they essentially provide  padding extra stability to the knee they  actually increase surface area of the  knee so you don't have one point of  pressure of the two bones rubbing on  each other i look down and my knee is  swollen up the size of a cantaloupe my  thing is green my leg is green here's  the normal egg here's the cream okay  that's not good i'm not sure if he's  having a blood clot happening there the  swelling is very worrisome to me that  would be like an urgent uh ultrasound a  duplex to see if that there is a clot  forming a lot of times when you're  sedated in a surgery you can have a  higher chance of developing a dvt which  is known as a deep venous thrombosis  where the blood being stagnant in your  veins because you're not moving you're  just laying down forms a clot the danger  of it if it gets dislodged and it  continues into the circulation it can  actually end up in your lungs block off  circulation in your lungs causing a  pulmonary embolism they said i still  [ __ ] out of it so all i did for a period  of five or six days was to sit on the  couch and ice my knee wow  video games are so cool the earlier we  can get even some slight motion  post-surgically in an area that was  operated on the better outcomes we get  and i'm not talking about him going and  starting squatting i'm talking about  just gentle mobility we actually even  have patients that have like robots and  machines do the motion for them just to  articulate the joint just to get that  fluid coming back into that area because  the longer we leave things locked up the  harder it is to unlock them after oh my  god look at that i have little tiny  holes in my knee so that goes to show he  did have arthroscopic surgery because he  had cameras inserted into those holes  and tools and you see that uh marker  there which is weird that they didn't  take that off that just shows that they  were operating on that knee before they  started operating that's how we know you  guys ready to see pictures of the inside  of my knee yeah you betcha this is like  the inside of my knee cavity they have  no idea what the acls and stuff and this  is a giant lump of scar tissue that they  shaved away you could have told me that  these were photos of your unborn child  and they're like oh my god she's  beautiful  the doctor told me you know it'll be a  week of being off your feet and then  i'll be back to normal i can help ariel  out because you know she's still  pregnant and it'll be great as the weeks  go on  my knee doesn't get a ton better today  i'm going to get my sutures taken out  they uh kept them in for an extra couple  of weeks it got through there's the  robot that i was talking about earlier  about getting mobility back into that  knee two weeks three weeks four weeks  and still i couldn't play soccer that's  not a surprise to me four weeks post-op  you're not playing soccer like that's  just the norm i sprained my ankle i  don't expect to play soccer in four  weeks time they gave me a prescription  to  painkillers  so here i am exactly in the place that i  didn't want to be  doing all the things that i didn't want  to do  i have a prescription full of  painkillers and i'm saying to them not  today  that is not the responsible way of  getting rid of painkillers there's  pharmacies that will take them back and  also if you're ever throwing away  medications like that rip your name off  the label just for privacy's sake or at  least black it out just so no one else  can get your info it's time for physical  therapy  look at this guy it's been resistance  bands not wearing many clothing you know  you could just see everything move a  little better i kept at it luckily ariel  was there yeah i mean i don't i honestly  couldn't have done it without ariel i  had the support of a great physical  therapist kept working kept icing it  slowly got better finally several months  after the recovery process  when wes was a  growing up before our very eyes  i got to play my first soccer game very  reasonable time frame out like honestly  when i talk to patients if they're  getting major knee surgery i would say  six to 12 months no soccer so seven  months is totally reasonable  i was able to run full speed on the  field cut back and forth kick the ball  yes sure my knee buckled a couple times  yes sure i wasn't perfect but i was back  to doing the things i enjoyed the most  markiplier also has made videos about  his medical journey click here to check  out my reaction to that and as always  stay happy and healthy i'll see you  markiplier's  [Music]

